<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    The debug build's one extra permission, and it is for the tests.

    A device test that reaches a real service has to be able to tell "this
    bench has no network" from "the screen is broken", and it has to tell them
    apart on a fact rather than on a wait - a skip decided by a timeout is how
    NewDiskTest once passed in twenty seconds having formatted nothing. The
    fact is ConnectivityManager.getActiveNetwork(), and asking it needs
    ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE: without it the call does not answer null, it throws
    SecurityException. See library/catalogue/CatalogueScreenTest.

    Here rather than in the main manifest because the app itself never asks the
    question - Http.Real tries and reports what happened, which is the right
    behaviour for a scrape and needs no permission at all. Nothing that ships
    carries this: the debug build type is the only one the instrumentation
    suite is ever installed on, and the release and Play manifests are
    untouched.

    ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE is a normal permission - granted at install, never
    prompted for, and it reveals nothing about the user.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE" />

</manifest>
